**Implicit Wait - Global Wait for All Elements**

**Definition:** Tells WebDriver to poll the DOM for a specified duration when finding elements.

**Key Characteristics:**
- Set **once** for entire driver session
- Applies **globally** to all `findElement()` calls
- Default timeout: 0 seconds (no wait)
- Polling interval: ~250-500ms (Selenium managed)

**Syntax:**
```java
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;
import java.time.Duration;

public class ImplicitWaitExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();

        // Set implicit wait - affects ALL findElement calls
        driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(Duration.ofSeconds(10));

        driver.get("https://example.com");

        // Will wait up to 10 seconds for element
        driver.findElement(By.id("username")).sendKeys("user");
        driver.findElement(By.id("password")).sendKeys("pass");
        // Both use the 10-second implicit wait

        driver.quit();
    }
}
```

**How It Works:**
```
1. findElement() called
2. Element found immediately? → Return
3. Element not found? → Poll DOM every 250-500ms
4. Keep polling until:
   - Element found → Return
   - 10 seconds elapsed → NoSuchElementException
```

**When to Use:**
✅ Simple applications with minimal dynamic content
✅ Quick prototyping and learning
✅ When elements have consistent load times

**When NOT to Use:**
❌ Complex dynamic applications
❌ When you need element state conditions (clickable, visible)
❌ Mixed with explicit waits (can cause issues)

**Important Notes:**
- Set it once at driver initialization
- Can be changed during session
- Use 0 to disable: `driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(Duration.ofSeconds(0));`

code:
// Configure implicit wait
driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(Duration.ofSeconds(10));

// Now ALL findElement calls wait up to 10 seconds
driver.findElement(By.id("element1")).click();
driver.findElement(By.id("element2")).click();

