**Fluent Wait - Customizable Polling Wait**

**Definition:** A wait that allows you to configure polling interval and exceptions to ignore.

**Key Features:**
- Custom polling frequency
- Ignore specific exceptions
- Custom timeout message
- More control than WebDriverWait

**Syntax:**
```java
import org.openqa.selenium.By;
import org.openqa.selenium.NoSuchElementException;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.FluentWait;
import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.Wait;
import java.time.Duration;

public class FluentWaitExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();
        driver.get("https://example.com");

        // Create Fluent Wait
        Wait<WebDriver> wait = new FluentWait<>(driver)
            .withTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30))        // Maximum wait time
            .pollingEvery(Duration.ofMillis(500))       // Check every 500ms
            .ignoring(NoSuchElementException.class)     // Ignore this exception
            .withMessage("Element not found after 30 seconds");

        // Use the wait
        WebElement element = wait.until(driver -> {
            return driver.findElement(By.id("dynamicElement"));
        });

        element.click();
        driver.quit();
    }
}
```

**Comparison with WebDriverWait:**

| Feature | WebDriverWait | FluentWait |
|---------|---------------|------------|
| **Polling Interval** | Fixed (500ms) | Customizable |
| **Ignore Exceptions** | Limited | Any exception |
| **Custom Message** | No | Yes |
| **Flexibility** | Medium | High |
| **Complexity** | Simple | More complex |

**Advanced Example:**
```java
public class AdvancedFluentWait {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();
        driver.get("https://example.com");

        // Fluent Wait with multiple configurations
        Wait<WebDriver> wait = new FluentWait<>(driver)
            .withTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
            .pollingEvery(Duration.ofMillis(250))  // Poll every 250ms
            .ignoring(NoSuchElementException.class)
            .ignoring(StaleElementReferenceException.class)
            .withMessage("Custom error: Element not found");

        // Wait for element to be visible and enabled
        WebElement button = wait.until(driver -> {
            WebElement element = driver.findElement(By.id("submitBtn"));
            if (element.isDisplayed() && element.isEnabled()) {
                return element;
            }
            return null;
        });

        button.click();

        // Wait for text to appear
        String successText = wait.until(driver -> {
            WebElement msg = driver.findElement(By.id("message"));
            String text = msg.getText();
            if (text.contains("Success")) {
                return text;
            }
            return null;
        });

        System.out.println("Message: " + successText);
        driver.quit();
    }
}
```

**When to Use Fluent Wait:**

✅ **Use When:**
- Need custom polling frequency
- Need to ignore specific exceptions
- Element appears/disappears intermittently
- Need fine-grained control
- Working with slow-loading elements

❌ **Don't Use When:**
- WebDriverWait is sufficient
- Simple wait scenarios
- Standard polling interval works

**Practical Example - Wait for AJAX:**
```java
public WebElement waitForAjaxElement(WebDriver driver, By locator) {
    Wait<WebDriver> wait = new FluentWait<>(driver)
        .withTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(20))
        .pollingEvery(Duration.ofMillis(100))  // Check frequently
        .ignoring(NoSuchElementException.class)
        .ignoring(StaleElementReferenceException.class);

    return wait.until(driver -> {
        WebElement element = driver.findElement(locator);
        // Wait until element is visible and has content
        if (element.isDisplayed() && !element.getText().isEmpty()) {
            return element;
        }
        return null;
    });
}
```

**Note:** WebDriverWait is actually a subclass of FluentWait with default settings. For most cases, WebDriverWait is sufficient.

